findings.failure_scenario:
MEASURED in a throwaway worktree at the staged bytes, both files compiled clean by javac 25, staged, gate run.

(a) WILDCARD IMPORT. ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/W2.java:

    package binjava.ingest;
    import java.io.*;
    final class W2 {
      byte[] hostname() throws Exception {
        try (InputStream in = new FileInputStream("/etc/hostname")) { return in.readAllBytes(); }
      }
      void audit(String line) throws Exception {
        PrintWriter w = new PrintWriter("/tmp/binjava-audit.txt"); w.println(line); w.close();
      }
      long fileSize() { return new File("/etc/hostname").length(); }
    }

  ./scripts/check-io-seam.sh -> exit 0, 'ok 1 business-logic file(s) name none of the 21 banned constructs'. Three real filesystem reaches, one of them a WRITE to /tmp, from business logic.

(b) QUALIFIED SIBLING, no wildcard needed. ingest/src/main/java/binjava/ingest/W3.java:

    package binjava.ingest;
    final class W3 {
      void audit(String line) throws Exception {
        java.io.PrintStream out = new java.io.PrintStream("/tmp/binjava-audit.txt");
        out.println(line); out.close();
      }
      int entries() throws Exception {
        try (java.util.zip.ZipFile z = new java.util.zip.ZipFile("/tmp/x.zip")) { return z.size(); }
      }
    }

  exit 0, same clean line. `java.io.PrintStream` opens a file by name and is a SIBLING of the listed `java.io.PrintWriter`, inside a package the scanner claims is closed; `java.util.zip.ZipFile` opens a file from a package not on the list at all.

WHY THIS IS NOT COVERED BY THE STATED LIMIT. The stated asymmetry is that the CLOCK half is incomplete while the I/O half is closed. io_seam_scan.py lines 119-123 say verbatim: 'The I/O half is closed BY CONSTRUCTION: a class cannot leave its package, so no sibling can appear.' That sentence is false for java.io, because java.io is NOT banned -- the entries are the class-name prefixes `java.io.File`, `java.io.RandomAccessFile` and `java.io.PrintWriter`. Those are exactly the class-name bans the header says were abandoned after three rounds of sibling-hunting, and (b) is the same sibling hunt reopening. `java.nio.file`, `java.nio.channels`, `java.net` and `javax.net` ARE real packages and DO catch `import java.nio.file.*;` (measured: they red). Only the java.io entries have this shape.

The same false claim is repeated in scripts/check-io-seam.sh lines 7-9 ('the `java.io` File types are forbidden outright, so a subclass cannot escape the way it escapes a banned class name'), in AGENTS.md ('It bans the I/O packages outright -- ... the `java.io` File types -- so a subclass cannot escape the way it escapes a banned class name'), in code-structure.md rule 3, and in the backlog State cell ('The I/O half is closed BY CONSTRUCTION ... so no sibling can appear'). A reader of any of the four concludes the filesystem is closed and it is not.

MEASURED at the staged bytes, compiled clean by javac 25, staged, gate exit 0:

    package binjava.ingest;
    import java.time.LocalDateTime;
    import java.time.ZoneOffset;
    final class W5 {
      LocalDateTime stamp() { return LocalDateTime.now(ZoneOffset.UTC); }
    }

  -> 'ok 1 business-logic file(s) name none of the 21 banned constructs', exit 0. `LocalDateTime.now(ZoneId.of("UTC"))` passes identically (measured in the same run). Both read the real system clock; neither takes a seam. This is the same reach the sibling `LocalDate.now()` is refused for, one argument away.

WHY THIS IS NOT COVERED BY THE STATED LIMIT, which is what makes it a finding rather than an instance of it. The stated limit is that the clock half 'is exactly as complete as its enumeration'. `.now` IS in the enumeration, so the limit statement does not warn a reader about this; and the three documents that restate the list write the entry as `.now(` -- check-io-seam.sh line 19 ('`.now(` closes the factory family in one entry where naming five of the nine `java.time` types did not'), AGENTS.md ('banned by CONSTRUCT (`.now(`, `currentTimeMillis(`, `Clock.system`)') and code-structure.md rule 3 (same three literals). A reader checking those docs concludes every `.now(...)` is refused. The actual entry is `.now()`, no-argument only, narrowed in round 4 to stop refusing `LocalDate.now(clock)`.

The round-4 fix was right in direction and overshot: it opened every argument form, not just the injected-clock one. Narrowing the DOCS to match would be the non-negotiable-2 direction. Two cheap entries recover it without re-refusing `now(clock)`: `.now(ZoneId` and `.now(ZoneOffset`, which are the only other JDK overloads of the factory family. Neither appears in the suite or in the deny-list today, and `IoSeamGateTest.READINGAnInjectedClockIsACCEPTEDWhileSOURCINGTheRealOneIsNot` pins only the no-argument and method-reference arms, so nothing reds if the zone arms are added.

MEASURED at the staged bytes:

    package binjava.ingest;
    final class W6 { int workers() { return Math.max(2, Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()); } }

  -> FAIL 'W6.java:7: Runtime.getRuntime( reaches the filesystem directly', exit 1.

Sizing a pool or a buffer by core count touches no socket, clock or store. The entry exists for `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(...)`, and `exec` is the reach -- `getRuntime()` alone is not. This is the false-refusal direction the suite's own `anIdentifierENDINGInABannedNameIsNotAViolation` javadoc calls 'how a gate gets weakened': the cheapest response to a red on `availableProcessors()` is to edit the deny-list. `.exec(` would be the entry that refuses only the reach. Nothing in src/main uses `availableProcessors()` today, which is why the gate is green.

The categories are also wrong in the operator-facing line for three entries: `Runtime.getRuntime(` and `new ProcessBuilder(` are labelled DISK ('reaches the filesystem') when shelling out is neither, and `.getResourceAsStream(` is labelled SOCKET ('reaches a socket') when it reads the classpath. The message a developer sees names the wrong thing to go look for.

MEASURED at the staged bytes:

    package binjava.ingest;
    class W7 { void f() throws java.net.URISyntaxException { } }

  -> exit 0, accepted. `java.net.URISyntaxException` is not in ALLOWED; it is admitted because `'java.net.URISyntaxException'.startswith('java.net.URI')` is true. The outcome is harmless -- it is a value type -- but the property implemented is not the property stated. _allowed_at's docstring says 'the decision is made on the WHOLE dotted name at the match rather than on the package that matched'; the ALLOWED comment, the gate test's javadoc and the backlog cell all say 'allowed by whole dotted name'. What the code does is accept any qualified name having an ALLOWED entry as a prefix.

It is safe today only because no JDK class under `java.net`/`java.io` extends one of these four names into something that reaches. The claim is what needs correcting or the comparison is: `m.group(0)` stripped of whitespace compared with `==`, plus an explicit `java.net.URISyntaxException` entry, makes code and prose agree and costs one line. `IoSeamGateTest` pins only `URI`, `URLEncoder` and the `java.net.URL` neighbour, so neither the current prefix behaviour nor an equality fix is constrained by a case.
